The UI-first Codex preview replan is recorded by [E-M17-081](milestones.md). It
defines a separate usable-preview gate and defers additional harnesses and
extended roadmap gates. The unfinished OpenCode import draft is bookmarked
outside the preview line. Live ancestry inspection found 51 committed main
changes, 80 capture changes and 40 overlapping paths after their common
ancestor; main's current working change remains separate. Main M18 is desktop,
requiring explicit task mapping during capture integration. Only documentation
checks qualify this replan. Rebase, automatic UI freshness, runnable setup and
real Codex/service/browser/restart acceptance remain pending and MUST be
recorded before preview completion. Existing library/test evidence is not that
acceptance.

Exact OpenCode API history preparation is recorded by
[E-M17-080](milestones.md). The regression selects a message-count/native-prefix
boundary with a second independent verification pass, preserves private receipts
after reopen and tail append, and refuses rewritten/truncated/rebound history.
Empty sessions retain zero messages and zero API byte offset. Selection,
reconciliation and journal validation grant no activity, source enrollment or
native publication. Shared snapshot hashes retain their original bytes. Public
historical selectors, worker import/consent renewal, exact receipt-backed API
completion, configured OpenCode startup, exported-file ingestion, Cursor and
M18.3-M17.7 remain open. M18/M18.2 remain In progress; no real harness/RustFS or
release claim follows from controlled loopback API tests.

# Verification

Ordinary local Stacks core delivery is recorded by [E-M21-040](milestones.md).
Real jj-lib fixtures qualify local TOML, merge/WIP membership, independent
unfinished work, stale admission, moved branch tips, native conflicts and linked
physical sparse checkouts. The reviewed UI uses the registered local command
route and existing pinned reads; future commit/file/hunk and graph actions
remain disabled. This checkpoint pauses for review before those engines. The
retained executor below is historical source/evidence and is not the production
path for ordinary local Stacks.

Stable physical stack-recovery admission is recorded by
[E-M21-039](milestones.md). Four new provider test parents prove agreement with
the existing observer, unchanged reads, independence from missing
operation/head/ index stores and damaged checkout/policy/editor state, distinct
identity after root or `.jj` replacement, unsafe/missing/oversized path refusal,
replacement during authorization and revoked access at each boundary. The
complete existing current-reader binary retains its five prior regressions. This
qualifies the provider primitive only; registered runtime composition, private
production paths, HTTP and writable UI remain incomplete. Work pauses after this
slice for the requested implementation discussion.

Bounded directory-aware OpenCode discovery is qualified by
[E-M17-076](milestones.md). The exact before target missed both linked native
workspaces when the default API instance status map was empty. Controlled
loopback API and native MemoryStore fixtures now publish both repositories with
independent busy/retry evidence. Unbound/unsafe directories receive no activity
request; absent native Write authority grants no source identity or admission.
Project and directory inventories reject malformed/oversized lists and filter
unsafe paths before context queries. Existing admitted sources recover offline
tails after restart even while new project enumeration returns unavailable.
Duplicate candidates are bounded per cycle; metadata cannot prove running
activity or grant inactive-history import.

The read profile follows pinned OpenCode 1.18.30 project/session handlers,
including the explicit global-project directory persistence omission. Global
(non-Git) candidates therefore use the bounded project-scoped session metadata
list; reaching its limit reports incomplete coverage. One owned step processes
at most one inventory/directory. These synthetic fixtures qualify bounded
retained-library discovery and its existing lifecycle/source/supervisor
consumers, separately from real harness/RustFS or whole-server release gates.
Configured startup remains withheld pending management/import/export, identity
joins and real-harness acceptance. M18/M18.2 remain In progress; later gates
remain Planned. No wire or durable metadata format changed. UI, unrelated Rust,
public contracts and root aggregate lanes remain out of scope.

OpenCode lifecycle checkpoint/recovery is qualified by
[E-M17-075](milestones.md). Controlled loopback API and native MemoryStore
fixtures prove active-to-idle publication for empty sessions without fictional
messages, exact busy/message intent recovery against a later idle API, repeated
status deduplication, retry projection, older message-only checkpoint upgrades,
spool pressure and current permission/catalog checks. Rewritten message prefixes
retain exact status evidence while withholding unavailable original messages.
Returning witnesses reconstruct their original generation and historical
lifecycle above newer channel allocation floors. The private optional checkpoint
separates native message prefix/count from lifecycle; its composite digest
advances source sequence without replaying messages. Existing None metadata and
Codex reader encodings remain unchanged.

Direct source/supervisor and reader-progress consumers are checked separately
from real harness/RustFS or configured whole-server release acceptance. No event
stream, missed intermediate state or globally consistent API snapshot is
claimed. Configured OpenCode startup stays withheld pending directory-aware
discovery, import/export/publication management, identity joins and real-harness
gates. M18/M18.2 remain In progress; later roadmap gates remain Planned. Root
aggregate, UI and unrelated package lanes are out of scope under targeted
verification.

Retained OpenCode library supervision for JST-COLLAB-017/JST-COLLAB-018/
JST-COLLAB-019/JST-COLLAB-030 is qualified by [E-M17-074](milestones.md).
Controlled loopback API and native MemoryStore fixtures prove automatic active
backfill, pause/resume, ended-session offline restart and bounded following.
Codex and OpenCode share one configured reader without starvation, and settled
API sources release their raw acquisition/cursor resources. Separate API
publication retains exact body-free entry fingerprints and existing exclusive
leases. A blocked native publication leaves source ingress responsive and keeps
its drain owner until release; changed sanitized metadata cannot borrow that
proof. Revoked repository partitions yield without reordering or discarding
prior intent. Source coverage joins the generation/ordinal catalog in selector
order, while API sources remain excluded from unqualified filesystem import
targets. API delivery and Codex delivery use separate proof paths over the same
sanitized spool and native authority.

The shared scheduler/status change requires the owning server library and the
direct capture supervisor/runtime/management/source/API consumers. It is a
retained-library qualification, separate from configured whole-server startup,
lifecycle projection, cross-workspace discovery, import/export/publication
management, hook/API aliases and real OpenCode/RustFS release acceptance.
Configured process startup still rejects the OpenCode profile. Internal status
adds the known API profile to existing dynamic harness/source serialization; no
public wire format is changed. UI, unrelated Rust packages and root aggregate
lanes remain out of scope. M18/M18.2 remain In progress, with later roadmap
gates Planned.

The private OpenCode recovery index is qualified by [E-M17-073](milestones.md).
Endpoint-scoped discovery recovers admitted native session IDs after a fresh
process exit before the first enqueue. Enumeration is read-only and cannot
allocate admission or authorize a changed directory witness. The existing
catalog atomically retains validated IDs and only hashed endpoint/directory
bindings, with no URL, path, body or opaque acquisition cursor. Corrupted,
foreign-version, duplicate and unbound entries fail closed; the index refuses
capacity without eviction. Older version-1 catalogs with no optional index
remain readable; exact admitted reacquisition populates metadata without
inventing an ID from a digest. The focused set covers the new
fresh-process/corruption tests, all source-registry units and the existing
registry/ingress consumers. Automatic OpenCode supervision and lifecycle
qualification remain pending; startup remains withheld. These private-filesystem
tests are separate from real harness/RustFS acceptance and do not complete
M18.2.

The admitted OpenCode API source path is qualified separately by
[E-M17-072](milestones.md). Private source registration, sanitized per-record
snapshot ingress, bounded raw-prefix reconstruction and exact native publication
run through the current AgentStore. The selected set covers three bridge tests,
three native parser tests, six existing source-registry tests, four existing
sanitization tests (plus its fresh-process child), five new source tests, four
existing Codex source tests and five existing read-only API tests. These are 30
selected top-level tests, rather than a workspace suite.

The controlled loopback source and native MemoryStore tests retain exclusive
endpoint/session ownership, current per-message repository admission, sanitized
secrets/home/temp/workspace paths, safe unbound omissions, durable
acknowledgement and exact restart without duplicate enqueue. They publish
selected native AgentStore records in two repositories and verify recorded
content and unchanged code heads. Unbound omission coverage belongs to the
source and cannot invent session activity. Idle history cannot enroll; revoked
catalog/Write/API-directory bindings and spool pressure retain exact intent
without advancing source state. An early queued-prefix rewrite regression fails
before its fix and starts a new monotonic generation afterward, retaining old
unresolved intents. Stable prefix reconstruction also ingests an appended
offline tail after explicit refresh.

This is an explicit source-driver qualification using synthetic versioned native
API responses, private real filesystem state and native MemoryStore. It does not
qualify live OpenCode, full cold server startup, real RustFS, cross-workspace
API discovery, foreground supervision/control/import management, lifecycle
status, hook/API identity joins or streaming exports. The existing startup gate
continues to withhold OpenCode. Snapshot acknowledgements are not the existing
JSONL byte publication-management inventory. Strict checks apply to both changed
Rust packages and their direct consumers; browser/root aggregates, public
contract generation and unrelated suites are out of scope. M18/M18.2 remain In
progress.

The pinned OpenCode acquisition/normalization boundary is qualified separately
by [E-M17-071](milestones.md). Eleven normalizer tests, three memory-only parser
tests and five controlled real-loopback HTTP tests preserve native message/tool
identities, explicit API lifecycle evidence and per-assistant directory context.
They reject wrong owners/versions, duplicate IDs, malformed/oversized pages,
redirects, changed directory bindings and repeated cursors. Exported messages
cannot prove current execution; missing assistant context cannot inherit the
session header directory. Source growth/timestamps are not activity evidence.

The read-only adapter is pinned to OpenCode 1.18.30 source
`3104c1428ec91f809e5ab86631300de41eb6952e`. It uses credential-free loopback
GETs and opaque X-Next-Cursor paging; acquisition progress does not acknowledge
a durable reader cursor. Strict lint and direct server compilation pass. These
fixtures qualify neither a running OpenCode worker nor complete exported JSON
streaming, cross-workspace discovery, durable ingestion, restart or real-edit
acceptance. Configured startup still withholds OpenCode. M18/M18.2 remain In
progress; later gates remain Planned. UI/root aggregates and unrelated Rust
suites are out of scope for this bounded Rust boundary.

Accepted-import restart recovery for JST-COLLAB-017/JST-COLLAB-018/
JST-COLLAB-030 is qualified separately in [E-M17-068](milestones.md). The
original exact regression failed to publish a continuing tail after a new
runtime without another command. Recovery now offers the original accepted
request to the exact rediscovered source, preserving its immutable receipt.
Restart fixtures cover durable pause/resume, revoked and restored repository
binding, physical-file replacement, disabled/missing state and cancellation of
fair policy acquisition while management still holds its gate.

The fresh-process prepared foreground fixture creates existing accepted intent
before enrollment, prepares actual private factory resources, binds management,
starts the retained runtime and requires completed native publication within its
original ten-second deadline while continuously polling public progress. Initial
fixture failures exposed a Write-only management configuration and repeated
nonblocking gate contention. Qualified Admin configuration plus fair queued
owned policy acquisition and permit retention fix recovery, preparation and
renewal without extending the deadline. The fixture also proves unchanged
accepted receipt and ordered storage lifetime. Cache warming precedes the
measured runtime; this is cold process/resource wiring, not cold sanitizer
compilation.

This Rust lifecycle slice runs the complete affected manager, supervisor,
runtime and executor modules; direct native source/supervisor/runtime and actual
management HTTP/native CLI consumers; prepared control/management cold targets;
and server all-target check and strict lint. Tests execute serially with
original scenario concurrency and deadlines. No public schema, generated-client
or persistence format changes require rerunning the complete unrelated HTTP/SSE
inventory. Browser/catalog/root aggregates and unrelated Rust suites remain out
of scope. These synthetic versioned Codex/native MemoryStore and private-file
results do not qualify enabled whole-server startup, browser management,
additional readers or real harness/RustFS release. M18.2 remains In progress.

The accepted-import progress/HTTP/CLI flow for JST-COLLAB-017/JST-COLLAB-018/
JST-COLLAB-030 is qualified by the selected targets recorded in
[E-M17-067](milestones.md). Before regressions show the old whole-history
projection discarding a proven accepted boundary after unknown tail history and
opening pending bodies during inspection. Metadata inspection now preserves a
qualified accepted boundary, refuses earlier holes/missing history, ignores
pending bodies, and neither recovers temporary catalog files nor creates missing
locks. A third before regression proves that a fresh import could incorrectly
link rewritten physical bytes to old native publication history.
Original-prefix/native admission now verifies the retained reader checkpoint
before selecting a verified reader association. Capture tests check mismatched
bytes and the shared four-MiB scan allowance. A fourth before regression shows
same-snapshot advancement restarting checkpoint scans and starving publication;
incremental hash extension now preserves the bound and invalidates on snapshot
changes. The original large-transcript worker regression passes without
extending its deadline. Additive older links stay unqualified until renewed
proof, without changing original generation/receipt. Native HTTP captures and
the fresh native CLI fixture cover durable acceptance, original-input
acknowledgement, exact replay, accepted publication, separate paused/stopped
tail phase, transcript removal, changed reuse, strict actor-free input and
current authority. Missing reader links, corrupt publication evidence and
rebound/missing state withhold completion without repair or owners. CLI
transport regressions reject foreign receipts, impossible completion and unknown
response fields without leaking arbitrary diagnostics or retrying.

This flow affects the capture metadata boundary, retained source settlement,
service/HTTP/CLI and prepared foreground resource consumers. Run focused
publication/progress, management library, management HTTP and native CLI suites;
retain activation/runtime/source cold consumers and five-package all-target
check/lint. Additive public registration requires the entire captured HTTP/SSE
inventory, generated schema/TypeScript and browser type checks. Suites retain
their original deadlines and scenario concurrency and execute serially. The
existing pinned sanitizer cache avoids rebuilding its native rule database; this
remains a fresh runtime test, not cold-cache sanitizer qualification. Native
MemoryStore and synthetic harness/device-file evidence do not establish real
RustFS/live harness release, browser management, enabled startup or automatic
accepted-request restart dispatch. The separately qualified recovery evidence
above covers only retained composition; other release gates remain pending.

M14 capture-coordination evidence separates durable queue behavior from live
publication qualification. Native publisher fixtures pause preparation and
publication while fresh child processes enqueue; an abruptly killed drainer
releases its lease and preserves its exact prepared request. The local provider
fixture covers the same enqueue/cancellation boundary and matching receipt
replay. Legacy exclusion, byte/count limits and symlink failures remain explicit
regressions. These tests qualify the shared drain foundation, not the future
service-owned recovery worker or agent Settings controls.

Planned rows name a gate, not completed evidence. Mark Implemented only after
passing concrete tests linked from the progress ledger.

